# net/refused.poke -- every way the socket ops say no.
#
# As with the filesystem refusals, the conversation CONTINUES THROUGH ALL OF
# THEM: each status here is recoverable, the error reply carries no payload,
# and the stream stays in step.
#
# Two of these are worth reading twice. Family 3 Unix answers NOTSUP rather
# than working, because a Unix socket address in this ABI is a preopened
# directory handle plus a relative path, FreeBSD has bindat and connectat
# which take exactly that, and Linux has neither -- an op needing a different
# mechanism on the two platforms is the thing this project will not ship. And
# connect on a LISTENER is refused from the handle's kind, before any syscall,
# because the two kernels do not agree on which errno that is.
#
# Frames transcribed by hand from ABI.md sections 4, 6 and 7.5 to 7.9.
#
#   hello  op 01, len 10
EMIT 01 0a 00 42 53 54 4d 01 00 00 00 00 00
READ 107
#   socket with domain 9 -- INVAL 06
EMIT 05 03 00 09 01 00
READ 3
#   socket with domain 3 Unix -- NOTSUP 17, declared and not built
EMIT 05 03 00 03 01 00
READ 3
#   socket with protocol 1 -- INVAL 06.  There is exactly one protocol per
#   domain and type this ABI can express.
EMIT 05 03 00 01 01 01
READ 3
#   socket with type 9 -- INVAL 06
EMIT 05 03 00 01 09 00
READ 3
#   a real socket.  Handle 4: 1, 2 and 3 are always the broker's stdio.
EMIT 05 03 00 01 01 00
READ 7
#   connect to 127.0.0.1 port 1, where nothing listens -- CONNREFUSED 20.
#   A pinned status rather than an errno: ECONNREFUSED is 61 on FreeBSD and
#   111 on Linux, and a program branching on the raw number would behave
#   differently on the two.
EMIT 06 26 00 04 00 00 00 00 00 01 00 01 00 7f 00 00 01
EMIT 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00
EMIT 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00
READ 3
#   accept on a socket that was never listened on -- BADF 03, from the kind
EMIT 09 06 00 04 00 00 00 00 00
READ 3
#   close it; the slot frees and its generation moves on
EMIT 0c 04 00 04 00 00 00
READ 3
#   a second socket takes the same slot back, so its handle is 0x00010004
EMIT 05 03 00 01 01 00
READ 7
#   listen on it.  The kind becomes listener and READ, WRITE and CONNECT are
#   dropped from its rights -- rights only ever narrow.
EMIT 08 06 00 04 00 01 00 00 00
READ 3
#   connect on the listener -- ISCONN 26
EMIT 06 26 00 04 00 01 00 00 00 01 00 01 00 7f 00 00 01
EMIT 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00
EMIT 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00
READ 3
#   read from the listener -- DENIED 04, because listen took the right away.
#   One byte rather than eight, like every other read in bf/net and bf/proc:
#   this one is refused before it reaches a stream at all, but the rule has no
#   exceptions so that a checker can enforce it without knowing which handles
#   are streams.
EMIT 0a 08 00 04 00 01 00 01 00 00 00
READ 3
#   close the listener
EMIT 0c 04 00 04 00 01 00
READ 3
#   exit  op 02, len 1, code 0
EMIT 02 01 00 00
READ 3
